  "query_preview": "..\n(Tuesday night through Sunday)\nIssued at 1246 AM MDT Mon Aug 24 2026\n\nConvection from storms in the east on Tuesday will send outflow\nboundaries westward into central and western parts of the state\nTuesday night increasing humidities there enough for increased\ncoverage of showers and thunderstorms Wednesday. The center of\nhigh pressure aloft will also sink over the southwest NM border on\nWednesday as a somewhat strong shortwave trough passes\nsoutheastward over the northeast half of the state. Shear and\ninstability will again be favorable for severe storms along and\neast of the Sangre de Cristo Mountains on Wednesday. PWATs will \nremain elevated over the east as well with a continued risk of\nlocally heavy rainfall and flash flooding from the Sangre de\nCristo Mountains eastward, where NBM 90th percentile QPF suggest a\nfew of the stronger storms will again be capable of producing \nover 1 inch of rainfall. \n\nBehind Wednesday's upper level trough some drier air will begin to\nfilter over the state from the northwest with a downtick in storm\ncoverage on Thursday over northwest and west central areas.\nElsewhere, scattered to isolated showers and storms will return\nThursday afternoon and evening, but rain intensity should decrease\nsome. Downward trend in storm coverage and rainfall intensity will\ncontinue areawide on Friday as the high pressure system aloft\nflattens out and elongates along an east-west ridge axis over \nsouthern NM. However, models depict the high pressure center \nshifting eastward over NM Saturday, and all the way to the \nMississippi River Valley Sunday, allowing better monsoon moisture \nfor thunderstorms to travel north and northeastward over the \nforecast area.\n\nTemperature-wise, record and near-record high temperatures will\ncontinue daily through the end of the work week, despite a few to\n5 degrees of cooling east of the continental divide on Wednesday.\nTemperatures will begin to trend upward as drier air moves in\nThursday, then continue to do so most places through Saturday,\nbefore trending cooler with the increased monsoon moisture over\ncentral and western areas on Sunday.",
